    <Label Name="Title" Style="Heading" Text="Inside a Raindrop" Pin="TopRight" OffsetX="16" OffsetY="16" WrapWidth="400" Backdrop="true" />
    <Label Name="Description" Style="Explanation" Text="Sunlight goes into a drop of rain, bounces off the back of it and comes out again, heading back toward the sun. On the way in and on the way out it bends, and every color bends by its own amount, so the colors come out a little apart.\n\nSlide the sun up and down to send its light into another part of the drop. However you aim, the red light never comes out at more than 42° from the way back to the sun, and violet never more than 40.5°. Show many rays at once: right at that largest angle they pile up and shine brightest. That is where you see the rainbow.\n\nSome light bounces twice before it gets out. It makes a second, fainter rainbow outside the first, with its colors the other way round." Pin="TopRight" OffsetX="16" OffsetY="64" WrapWidth="400" Backdrop="true" />
  </Figures>
</Drawing>